          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-c613a8f4abbc3670\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"The imagined “Sì, ti amo!” is principally an avowal presenting Arrigo’s love for Loretta as true. The continuation about her being his first and only possible love gives slight support to relational commitment, but the core phrase does not clearly undertake one. Although his emotions are intense and the words resound internally, the passage frames them as a contemplated answer that shame prevents him from uttering, not as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The claims of unique and enduring love create limited performative pressure, but they read more naturally as descriptions of the scope of his feeling than as a promise or undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"imagined\",\"description\":\"Arrigo wishes he could answer Loretta with “Sì, ti amo!”, but he is ashamed and remains silent; the utterance occurs as an internally formulated, unrealised reply.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Arrigo’s imagined avowal concerns his sister Loretta and is framed by intense desire, intoxication, shame, self-horror, and awareness of forbidden incestuous love. Those circumstances explain both the passion of the formulation and his inability to voice it. They support the sincerity and emotional intensity of the represented avowal but do not independently make its core force expressive/reflexive or performative.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“avrebbe voluto a sua volta, risponderle: «Sì, ti amo!»”\",\"supports\":\"Identifies the words as an imagined desired reply and directly supports strong truth-conditional avowal force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“sei la prima che amo, la sola che potrò mai amare”\",\"supports\":\"Presents his love as unique and enduring, strongly reinforcing T and giving limited support to a commitment-like interpretation.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ma di questo si vergognava, e le parole che suonavan dentro gli parevano impossibili a dirsi. Allora taceva”\",\"supports\":\"Shows that the utterance remains internal and unrealised; its considered formulation and suppression weigh against treating it as a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration identifies Loretta as Arrigo’s full sister and describes his love as forbidden, guilty, and accompanied by self-horror.\",\"supports\":\"Explains the shame, silence, and extreme affect surrounding the avowal without requiring an additional category of core force.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the imagined avowal, with slight P support from its language of exclusive enduring attachment. Emotional intensity, internal resonance, and inhibition are adequately represented as context and do not leave residual expressive or other force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification concerns Arrigo’s imagined “Sì, ti amo!”, not Loretta’s separately reported declarations of love.\"}"
        }
